=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The helpdesk ticket's premise is incorrect — both Campus 2 gateways are fully reachable and the network is healthy. Direct measurement shows that pc1 (10.5.0.1) and pc2 reach 10.5.0.254 with 0% packet loss and TTL=64 (one hop, direct L2 delivery to the SVI), and pc3 (10.6.0.3) and pc4 reach 10.6.0.254 identically. Cross-VLAN routing also functions: pc1 reaches pc3 via a routed hop (TTL=63), confirming sshswitch is forwarding between br_v5 and br_v6 correctly.

Inspection of sshswitch confirms that the two gateway SVIs — br_v5 (10.5.0.254/24) and br_v6 (10.6.0.254/24) — are present, UP, and assigned their correct addresses. The VLAN subinterfaces bond0.5, bond0.6, eth0.5, and eth0.6 are all members of the respective bridge domains. The trunk link (eth0, ms1_sw4) between sshswitch and switch4 is also UP, delivering tagged VLAN 5 and VLAN 6 traffic correctly.

Root cause: There is no fault. The user's claim that "the big switch came back up without the gateways" is a false belief, likely caused by a transient connectivity issue at the time of the reload that has since self-resolved, or by end-host ARP/default-route problems on specific workstations that do not affect the lab infrastructure.

Corrective fix: No changes are needed on sshswitch or any other network device. Inform the reporter that both gateways are responding normally and ask affected users to release/renew their DHCP leases or flush their ARP caches, as the transient reload may have left stale state on individual workstations.
=== END ===